 ],
 "literal": "Like the leg of a quail, the red-stemmed black gram's / ripened mature pods, the herd of deer consumes, in this / harsh cold early winter season, to cure [the chill], / other medicine there is none, except his embraced chest indeed.",
 "translation": "To cure the harsh cold of this early winter season, when herds of deer feed on the mature, ripened pods of the red-stemmed black gram that look like the legs of quails, there is no other medicine than his chest that once embraced me.",
 "modern": "காடைப் பறவையின் காலைப் போன்ற சிவந்த தண்டுடைய உளுந்தின் முற்றிய நெற்றுகளை மான்கூட்டம் கவராநிற்கும் அரிய பனியையுடைய இக்கூதிர் காலத்துத் துன்பத்தைத் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து, அவர் தழுவிய மார்பைத் தவிர வேறு ஒன்றும் இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "In this poem, the heroine (talaivi) speaks to her companion (tōḻi) about her suffering during the cold early winter season (arciram) in the absence of her lover. She describes a vivid scene from nature where herds of deer graze on the dark, ripe pods of the red-stemmed black gram, which resemble the reddish legs of a quail. This imagery of deer feeding together highlights the warmth of companionship in nature, contrasting sharply with her own loneliness. The biting cold of the winter represents the emotional chill of separation. She concludes that the only 'medicine' (maruntu) capable of curing this deep physical and emotional cold is the warm embrace of her lover's chest.",
